import{_ as t,o,c as s,a2 as i}from"./chunks/framework.k9nOhppk.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"SPECS.md review, 2026-10-12","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"internals/reviews/spec-review-2026-10-12.md","filePath":"internals/reviews/spec-review-2026-10-12.md","lastUpdated":1791864160000}'),n={name:"internals/reviews/spec-review-2026-10-12.md"};function d(a,e,r,c,l,h){return o(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<h1 id="specs-md-review-2026-10-12" tabindex="-1">SPECS.md review, 2026-10-12 <a class="header-anchor" href="#specs-md-review-2026-10-12" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;SPECS.md review, 2026-10-12&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A broad, critical review of SPECS.md as a language specification, written by a reviewing agent on 2026-10-12 against <code>dev</code> at <code>2020c6e</code> (SPECS.md was 2,051 lines). Line numbers are SPECS.md&#39;s at that commit unless a file is named; they drift as SPECS changes. Findings marked <strong>[verified]</strong> were reproduced with a scratch program; findings marked <em>(unsure)</em> are inferences. The conclusions drawn from it, and what to do about each finding, are in <a href="./conclusions-2026-10-12">conclusions-2026-10-12.md</a>.</p><p>Some findings were fixed after the review ran: G.4 to G.6 (<code>Type.f()</code> through a module, a self-naming alias, an <code>impl</code> on an entity), C.3 (schedules), 6.3 (<code>@fresh</code> and <code>@destructive</code>, which now gives <code>Version</code> a type) and 6.13 (serving contracts over HTTP) landed the same day.</p><hr><h2 id="top-10-findings-ranked" tabindex="-1">Top 10 findings, ranked <a class="header-anchor" href="#top-10-findings-ranked" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Top 10 findings, ranked&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="_1-entity-methods-can-have-outside-effects-the-spec-contradicts-itself-and-the-code-follows-the-permissive-side-verified" tabindex="-1">1. Entity methods can have outside effects: the spec contradicts itself, and the code follows the permissive side [verified] <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-entity-methods-can-have-outside-effects-the-spec-contradicts-itself-and-the-code-follows-the-permissive-side-verified" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. Entity methods can have outside effects: the spec contradicts itself, and the code follows the permissive side [verified]&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>The spec says both things:</strong><ul><li>§12, L831–833: &quot;Outside jobs the same functions are called without <code>!</code>: in <code>main</code> …, in tests, <strong>and in entity methods and served operations</strong>.&quot;</li><li>§14, L1014–1018: &quot;<strong>Methods never wait.</strong> They can&#39;t use <code>!</code>, so they can&#39;t call other services; those calls belong in jobs… Because entity methods have no outside side effects, the runtime can safely retry one after a conflict.&quot;</li></ul></li><li><strong>What the code does:</strong> these all pass <code>eye check</code>, and the test runs: <ul><li>an entity method calling <code>notify.send(…)</code> (a contract command);</li><li><code>fs.writeText</code>, <code>now()</code> and <code>random.int</code> in an entity method;</li><li><code>sleep(1 s)</code> in an entity method, which blocks while the entity is held.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Why it matters:</strong> this breaks pillar 2 (atomic, retryable methods) and pillar 4 (keyed calls, never blindly retried). If a conflict retries the transaction, the network call or file write happens twice.</li><li><strong>Fix:</strong> keep §14&#39;s rule and make §12 say &quot;in <code>main</code>, in tests; in entity methods and served operations only starting a job and publishing, which commit with the transaction&quot;. Have the checker refuse <code>net</code>, <code>fs</code>, <code>process</code>, <code>clock</code>, <code>random</code>, <code>env</code>, <code>ffi</code>, <code>secret</code> and <code>wait</code> in entity methods and in <code>impl Contract</code> functions. If reading the clock inside a transaction is wanted, decide it separately.</li></ul><h3 id="_2-reading-an-entity-in-a-job-without-a-step-a-resumed-run-sees-a-different-value-verified" tabindex="-1">2. Reading an entity in a job without a step: a resumed run sees a different value [verified] <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-reading-an-entity-in-a-job-without-a-step-a-resumed-run-sees-a-different-value-verified" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. Reading an entity in a job without a step: a resumed run sees a different value [verified]&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>The promise:</strong> L1062–1063 says a resumed run &quot;sees the values it saw&quot;. L1019–1023 says a job reads an entity with <code>w.read!()</code>. <code>w.read()</code> without <code>!</code> in a job is correctly refused.</li><li><strong>What the code does:</strong><ul><li>A field read on a <code>Ref</code> (<code>let before = w.phase</code>) in a job compiles, and so do reads of a view or entity set (<code>busy |&gt; size</code>, <code>W.all |&gt; size</code>).</li><li>None of these is a recorded step.</li><li>In a test, a job read <code>w.phase</code> (Asleep), slept 10 minutes while the test changed it, then woke. The run replayed from its start, since no checkpoint had been taken yet, re-read the field and recorded <code>Running</code>. A run that keeps its own values should have recorded <code>Asleep</code>.</li></ul></li><li><strong>The spec is silent:</strong> it never says whether <code>ref.field</code> is legal (the tour&#39;s test uses <code>w.phase</code> at L209, outside a job). It also never says how a job reads a view.</li><li><strong>Fix:</strong><ul><li>State that <code>ref.field</code> outside jobs is shorthand for <code>ref.read().field</code>.</li><li>In a job, make <code>ref.field</code>, view reads and <code>E.all</code> either errors (&quot;read it with <code>w.read!()</code>&quot;) or recorded steps.</li></ul></li></ul><h3 id="_3-nested-entity-calls-that-come-back-to-an-entity-lose-a-write-verified" tabindex="-1">3. Nested entity calls that come back to an entity lose a write [verified] <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-nested-entity-calls-that-come-back-to-an-entity-lose-a-write-verified" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3. Nested entity calls that come back to an entity lose a write [verified]&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>The spec:</strong> L1005–1006 says &quot;A method may call methods on other entities, and the whole call tree commits or rolls back together.&quot; It says nothing about a call tree that returns to an entity already in it.</li><li><strong>The test:</strong> <code>A.ping</code> does <code>n += 1</code> and then calls <code>B(id).pong()</code>. <code>B.pong</code> calls <code>A(id).bump()</code>, which also does <code>n += 1</code>. Afterwards <code>A(&quot;x&quot;).read().n</code> is <strong>1, not 2</strong>. One update is silently lost.</li><li><strong>Fix:</strong> define re-entry. Either the inner call sees and updates the outer call&#39;s in-progress state, or calling back into an entity already in the call tree is an error with a message. Add a test either way.</li></ul><h3 id="_4-effects-missing-from-public-signatures-verified" tabindex="-1">4. Effects missing from public signatures [verified] <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-effects-missing-from-public-signatures-verified" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;4. Effects missing from public signatures [verified]&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Pillar 5 and L777–779 say &quot;A public function without a <code>uses</code> clause is pure, and the compiler holds it to that&quot;. These all pass <code>eye check</code>:</p><ul><li><strong>Entity methods:</strong> <code>pub fn</code> entity methods never need <code>uses</code>, even with <code>fs</code>, <code>clock</code> and <code>random</code> in the body. <code>impl T</code> methods do need it, and callers of entity methods do get the effects.</li><li><strong>Public jobs:</strong> a <code>pub job</code> that does <code>fs.readText!</code>, <code>now!()</code> and <code>sleep!</code> needs no <code>uses</code>. The grammar allows a <code>uses</code> clause on jobs, but the spec never says whether one is required.</li><li><strong>Starting a job:</strong> this is not an effect. <code>pub fn starter() -&gt; Run(()) { worker.start(1) }</code> passes as &quot;pure&quot;; §12&#39;s table has no row for it.</li><li><strong>Calling a waiting function without <code>!</code>:</strong> this drops <code>wait</code>. <code>pub fn pause() { sleep(1 s) }</code> passes as pure, and <code>main</code> can call <code>sleep(1 s)</code> without listing anything. (<code>ir/builtin.eye</code> says <code>sleep</code> blocks outside jobs; SPECS never does.)</li><li><strong>The key rule for jobs started from <code>main</code>:</strong> the rule at L888–890 checks only <code>main</code>&#39;s own body. <code>fn kick() { ring.start(&quot;kitchen&quot;) }</code> called from <code>main</code> passes, and starts a new run on every restart.</li></ul><p><strong>Fix:</strong> decide and write down whether entity methods and jobs carry <code>uses</code>. Add an effect for starting jobs (for example <code>start J</code>), which also lets the key rule follow calls. Say that a call to a function with <code>uses {wait}</code> outside a job either keeps the effect or is refused.</p><h3 id="_5-outside-jobs-and-waiting-functions-are-never-defined-verified" tabindex="-1">5. <code>!</code> outside jobs and &quot;waiting functions&quot; are never defined [verified] <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-outside-jobs-and-waiting-functions-are-never-defined-verified" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5. `!` outside jobs and &quot;waiting functions&quot; are never defined [verified]&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Row 104 (L104):</strong> &quot;<code>!</code> marks durable steps and is used only in jobs.&quot;</li><li><strong>§12, L752–754:</strong> the effects example is <code>pub fn summarise(…) uses {wait, net Llm.complete} { llm.complete!(…) }</code>, an ordinary function with a <code>!</code> call.</li><li><strong>§12, L759:</strong> <code>wait</code> is &quot;allowed only in jobs and <strong>waiting functions</strong>&quot;. The term is never defined.</li><li><strong>Elsewhere:</strong> L1067 (&quot;a function with <code>!</code> steps of its own&quot;) and open question 23 also assume such functions exist.</li><li><strong>What the code does:</strong> <code>!</code> is accepted in any function, <code>pub fn waiter() uses {wait} { sleep!(1 s) }</code> included. A job must then call it as <code>waiter!()</code>.</li><li><strong>Fix:</strong> add a short rule to §12. A <em>waiting function</em> is one whose body has <code>!</code> steps. It has <code>wait</code> in its effects, and only jobs and other waiting functions may call it, with <code>!</code>. Change row 104 to &quot;only in jobs and the functions they call&quot;. Say what <code>main</code> may do with such a function.</li></ul><h3 id="_6-ending-an-entity-is-undefined-and-end-ok-collides-with-a-built-in-verified" tabindex="-1">6. Ending an entity is undefined, and <code>end(ok)</code> collides with a built-in [verified] <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-ending-an-entity-is-undefined-and-end-ok-collides-with-a-built-in-verified" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;6. Ending an entity is undefined, and `end(ok)` collides with a built-in [verified]&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>The collision:</strong> L995&#39;s <code>pub fn retire(ok: Approval) { end(ok) }</code> resolves to the string function <code>end(s: String) -&gt; Offset</code> (<code>ir/builtin.eye</code> L442). The error is &quot;expected String, found Approval&quot;.</li><li><strong>The missing rules:</strong> the spec never says what an ended entity is. Entities are virtual (row 81, L1003: &quot;exists on first use&quot;), so the next <code>Player(id)</code> would recreate it from defaults. That means deletion with an Approval doesn&#39;t actually delete anything.</li><li><strong>Fix:</strong> use a name that can&#39;t collide, such as a method <code>self.end(ok)</code> or <code>retire</code>. Then define: <ul><li>a tombstone: a call on an ended entity returns an error or faults;</li><li>whether its <code>Ref</code> stays a valid value;</li><li>that views fire <code>leaves</code>;</li><li>what happens to stored rows and backups;</li><li>who checks the <code>Approval</code> (its <code>what</code>?).</li></ul></li></ul><h3 id="_7-results-and-command-outcomes-can-be-silently-thrown-away-verified" tabindex="-1">7. Results and command outcomes can be silently thrown away [verified] <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-results-and-command-outcomes-can-be-silently-thrown-away-verified" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7. Results and command outcomes can be silently thrown away [verified]&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>The tour does it:</strong> L192 <code>notify.send!(owner, …, key: …)</code> stands alone as a statement, so a <code>Refused</code> or <code>Uncertain</code> reply goes nowhere.</li><li><strong>The code allows it everywhere:</strong> <code>fs.writeText(&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;)</code> as a statement in <code>main</code> compiles. L1278–1280 only stops an <code>Uncertain</code> from being <em>passed on</em>; it can still be dropped.</li><li><strong>Why it matters:</strong> this is the easy-but-wrong program that pillar 4 and &quot;How to decide&quot; item 1 warn about.</li><li><strong>Fix:</strong> a statement whose value is a <code>Result</code> or a command&#39;s outcome must be used, matched or explicitly discarded (<code>let _ = …</code>). Put the rule in §10 and §17 and update the tour.</li></ul><h3 id="_8-stale-text-that-contradicts-the-code-or-the-rest-of-the-spec" tabindex="-1">8. Stale text that contradicts the code or the rest of the spec <a class="header-anchor" href="#_8-stale-text-that-contradicts-the-code-or-the-rest-of-the-spec" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;8. Stale text that contradicts the code or the rest of the spec&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>Says</th><th>Reality</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Row 96 (L96), §16 L1214</td><td>Pipelines over entity sets &quot;compile to SQL&quot;</td><td>Not built. ROADMAP &quot;What&#39;s next&quot; recommends saying they run in memory.</td></tr><tr><td>§22 L1722–1724</td><td>&quot;The interpreter resumes a job by running it again from the start&quot;</td><td>Since 7.3 it resumes from statement checkpoints (§15 L1063, row 131).</td></tr><tr><td>Row 80 (L80), §15 heading L1061</td><td>&quot;Every <code>!</code> call in a job is also a <strong>checkpoint</strong>&quot;</td><td>§15 L1063–1065: a checkpoint is taken at statements, once steps have been taken.</td></tr><tr><td>§15 L1096–1097</td><td>&quot;Until traits are checked, <code>Store</code> is the Go interface…&quot;</td><td>Traits are checked; <code>check/runtime/store.eye</code> L55 declares <code>trait Store(s)</code>.</td></tr><tr><td>Q24 L1972–1973</td><td>&quot;The decision log&#39;s own example of <code>dyn</code> is choosing a store&quot;</td><td>Row 110&#39;s example is <code>fn notifier(config) -&gt; dyn Notifier</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><code>@priority</code> row L1183</td><td>&quot;lower priorities pause at their next checkpoint&quot;</td><td>Q26 L1986–1988: nothing is paused mid-run.</td></tr><tr><td>Row 117 (L117)</td><td>&quot;(open question 32)&quot; for string positions</td><td>Q32 is time-travel debugging.</td></tr><tr><td>L3</td><td>&quot;decisions come from design sessions on 2026-10-10&quot;</td><td>Many rows are from 2026-10-11.</td></tr><tr><td>§21 L1657–1679</td><td>&quot;Proposed handling&quot;, &quot;Delete now&quot;, &quot;Before deleting anything, tag…&quot;</td><td>All done; Eyelang 1 is gone.</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Fix:</strong> do one sweep. Collapse §21 to two lines, and move dated and implementation notes to <code>docs/internals</code>.</p><h3 id="_9-examples-with-real-errors-not-just-missing-context-verified-with-eye-check" tabindex="-1">9. Examples with real errors (not just missing context) [verified with <code>eye check</code>] <a class="header-anchor" href="#_9-examples-with-real-errors-not-just-missing-context-verified-with-eye-check" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;9. Examples with real errors (not just missing context) [verified with `eye check`]&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>§8 L553</strong> <code>List(dyn Handler)</code>: the checker says &quot;dyn takes a trait, and Handler is a type&quot;. <code>Handler</code> is a built-in type (<code>ir/builtin.eye</code> L136, the type <code>with</code> takes), and §12 L803 also declares <code>type Handler = {…}</code>. Three meanings for one name.</li><li><strong>§10 L614–618</strong> <code>BadNumber(ParseError)</code>: there is no <code>ParseError</code>; <code>parseInt</code> returns <code>Result(Int, String)</code>.</li><li><strong>§10 L614–618 and §13 L864</strong> <code>FsError</code> unqualified: it must be <code>fs.FsError</code>. §13 fails even with <code>import fs</code>; §10 doesn&#39;t import <code>fs</code> at all.</li><li><strong>§11 L697</strong> <code>map(fn a: { id: &quot;{a}&quot;, label: &quot;{a}&quot; })</code>: &quot;which record type is this?&quot; The example breaks §5&#39;s nominal-record rule (L342–351).</li><li><strong>§12 L793–794</strong> <code>fs.readText(p) |&gt; size</code>: <code>size</code> of a <code>Result</code> is an error.</li><li><strong>§13 L956</strong> <code>fs.lines(path)</code>: no such function in <code>check/std/fs.eye</code>.</li><li><strong>§15 L1155</strong> publishes <code>{ id, approved, by }</code>, but <code>approvals.Answer</code> has <code>what</code> and no <code>id</code> (C.8/#71 isn&#39;t built). Mark it as the target design until it lands.</li><li><strong>§19 L1591–1592</strong> <code>codex.start!(…).result!()?</code>: nothing gives the result a type, and §19&#39;s own rule (L1509–1511) says &quot;When nothing says, write it&quot;.</li><li><strong>§19 L1558</strong> <code>tools: [searchIssues, label]</code>: a list of functions with different types, which no rule here can type.</li><li><strong>§11 L655</strong> <code>self.nick</code> on <code>Player</code>, but §14 L986 says <code>nick</code> was renamed to <code>name</code>. (<code>impl</code> on an entity was also ignored at the time; it is an error since G.6.)</li><li><strong>§15 L1088</strong> <code>World(w).markAsleep!()</code>, where <code>w</code> is already a <code>Ref(World)</code> in the tour. It should be <code>w.markAsleep!()</code>.</li><li><strong>§9 L574</strong> <code>receipt.age</code>, but §5 L353 makes <code>receipt</code> a <code>ReceiptId</code>.</li><li><strong>Tour, L160:</strong> <code>wake.start(id)</code> uses a job that is never shown. It, <code>PlayerId</code>, <code>SleepError</code> and <code>SleepRefused</code> live in <code>testdata/programs/tour/worlds.eye</code>; the spec should say so.</li><li><strong>Tour, L188:</strong> the comment &quot;exactly once: same transaction as the checkpoint&quot; sits right under <code>zeropod.checkpoint!</code>, so a reader takes &quot;checkpoint&quot; to mean zeropod&#39;s. See the terminology notes below.</li><li><strong>Inconsistent examples across sections:</strong><ul><li>Three incompatible Notify contracts: <code>send(to, text)</code> at L1229; <code>send(event, title, priority, to)</code> at L1343; <code>ask</code>/<code>answered</code> at L1149–1152.</li><li><code>summarise</code> is a pub fn at L752, a job at L1487, and <code>summarise!(items)</code> returning text at L1055.</li></ul></li></ul><h3 id="_10-rules-the-code-chose-that-specs-never-records-verified" tabindex="-1">10. Rules the code chose that SPECS never records [verified] <a class="header-anchor" href="#_10-rules-the-code-chose-that-specs-never-records-verified" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10. Rules the code chose that SPECS never records [verified]&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Built-in collection functions work as methods:</strong> <code>xs.first()</code>, <code>s.size()</code> and <code>s |&gt; chars</code> all compile. This contradicts L532–534 (&quot;<code>x |&gt; f(a)</code> calls an ordinary function; <code>x.f(a)</code> calls a method&quot;) and L1010–1011. User functions don&#39;t work this way (<code>3.twice()</code> is &quot;Int has no method twice&quot;). It also gives built-ins two spellings, against &quot;one obvious way&quot;.</li><li><strong>Imported variants work unprefixed everywhere:</strong> <code>fn t() = Blocked</code> compiles. §9 L597–599 grants this only to patterns.</li><li><strong>Entity methods&#39; lambdas may capture state fields</strong> (<code>filter(fn p: p != owner)</code>). §7 L491–492 forbids capturing <code>mut</code> parameters, and entity methods are <code>mut self</code> (L498, L1007).</li><li><strong>A <code>state version</code> field is silently replaced</strong> by the snapshot&#39;s transaction <code>version</code>: <code>read().version</code> gave 1, not 8. There is no checker error.</li><li><strong>Faults beyond the six listed (L1158–1159) become <code>Unreachable(msg)</code></strong> (<code>interp/jobs.go</code> L183–189). That covers a 4xx reply (L1359), a replay mismatch (L1079), a missing fake, and an unknown model price.</li><li><strong>Some standard modules need <code>import</code>, others don&#39;t:</strong> <code>fs</code>, <code>random</code>, <code>process</code>, <code>http</code>, <code>ai</code> and <code>approvals</code> do; <code>env</code> and <code>log</code> are ambient. The spec doesn&#39;t say which; §10 and §12 use <code>fs</code> and <code>random</code> without importing them.</li><li><strong>Integer ranges don&#39;t wrap, time ranges do:</strong> <code>5..2</code> over <code>Int</code> is empty, while <code>23:00..08:00</code> wraps (L362). <code>(0..=5).end</code> is 5, so the <code>end</code> field doesn&#39;t say whether a range includes it.</li></ul><p><strong>Fix:</strong> record each in its section, or change the code.</p><hr><h2 id="full-lists-by-category" tabindex="-1">Full lists by category <a class="header-anchor" href="#full-lists-by-category" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Full lists by category&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="_1-contradictions" tabindex="-1">1. Contradictions <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-contradictions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. Contradictions&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>Inside the spec</strong></p><ul><li>Entity methods and outside effects: §12 L831–833 against §14 L1014–1018 (top 1).</li><li><code>!</code> only in jobs (row 104) against waiting functions (L752, L759, L1067, Q23) (top 5).</li><li>What a checkpoint is: row 80 and L1061 against L1063 and row 131 (top 8).</li><li>Resume by replay from the start (§22 L1722) against checkpoints (§15) (top 8).</li><li>Function types without <code>uses</code>: <ul><li>L799: &quot;A function type without <code>uses</code> is pure&quot;.</li><li>L812–814: &quot;A parameter&#39;s function type without <code>uses</code>… takes a callback, which may do anything&quot;.</li><li>The same <code>fn(a) -&gt; b</code> means different things by position, and L799&#39;s sentence is wrong as written. (<code>grammar.js</code> L823 repeats &quot;is pure&quot;.)</li></ul></li><li>The newline rule: <ul><li>Row 119 continues a line that starts with &quot;an operator, <code>.</code>, <code>,</code>, <code>:</code>, <code>=</code> or <code>else</code>&quot;.</li><li>§4 L296 adds <code>?</code> and <code>-&gt;</code>.</li></ul></li><li>Braces: <ul><li>Row 120 lists what opens a block.</li><li>§4 L312–313 adds <code>let … else</code> and <code>test &quot;…&quot;</code>.</li></ul></li><li>Impls: <ul><li>§8 L525 and §11 L666 say an <code>impl</code> goes only in the module of the type or trait.</li><li>§17 L1312 serves <code>impl notify.Notify</code> from another module (the lab does this in <code>notifier.eye</code>). The exception for contracts is never stated.</li></ul></li><li>Ports: <ul><li>L1248–1249 / row 94: &quot;Callers never write a port; they get it from the contract, so no call can go to a wrong or undeclared one&quot;.</li><li>L1362–1363: the service&#39;s URL, which carries a port, comes from <code>NOTIFY_URL</code>.</li><li>Which one wins?</li></ul></li><li><code>@destructive</code>: <ul><li>On a command, it adds an <code>Approval</code> parameter (L1302).</li><li>On a function used as a model tool, it pauses for a person (L1550, L1564).</li><li>Same attribute, two meanings.</li></ul></li><li><code>@priority</code>&#39;s table row (L1183) against Q26&#39;s honest description.</li><li><code>@memory</code> and &quot;All of them are enforced by the runtime&quot; (L1188) against Q26.</li><li>Q24&#39;s premise against row 110.</li></ul><p><strong>Spec against code (verified, beyond the top 10)</strong></p><ul><li><code>?</code> wrapping: with two variants that both hold <code>String</code>, the message says &quot;none of its variants holds a String&quot; instead of &quot;several&quot;. This is a small checker bug.</li><li><code>@fresh</code>&#39;s <code>basedOn: Version</code> (L1300): there was no <code>Version</code> type; the snapshot&#39;s <code>version</code> was an <code>Int</code>. (6.3 has since made <code>Version</code> a type.)</li><li>Approvals by <code>id</code> and only by <code>from</code> (row 128, L1130–1139) aren&#39;t built. The prelude says answers match by text; status.md marks it partial (#71). The spec text should say it is decided and not yet built.</li><li><code>check/prelude.eye</code> L98 still says &quot;a Secret, <strong>a Handle</strong> and a function have none&quot;; <code>Handle</code> is gone (row 130).</li></ul><h3 id="_2-gaps-rules-a-reader-or-implementer-needs" tabindex="-1">2. Gaps (rules a reader or implementer needs) <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-gaps-rules-a-reader-or-implementer-needs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. Gaps (rules a reader or implementer needs)&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Ended entities</strong> (top 6).</li><li><strong>Re-entrant entity calls</strong> (top 3).</li><li><strong>Reading entities and views in jobs</strong> (top 2).</li><li><strong>Retrying a transaction after a conflict:</strong> what happens to effects it already did? (This only matters if top 1 isn&#39;t fixed.)</li><li><strong>Which effects public items must state:</strong> entity methods, jobs, <code>start</code>, wait-effect calls without <code>!</code> (top 4).</li><li><strong>Discarded values:</strong> no rule at all (top 7).</li><li><strong>Faults:</strong><ul><li>which <code>Fault</code> a 4xx reply, a replay mismatch, a missing fake or an unknown price produces;</li><li>what a fault means outside a job: a query unavailable for a minute (L1292–1293) or a 4xx in <code>main</code>, a test or an entity method — &quot;the run ends&quot;, but there is no run.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Events have no caller guard.</strong> <code>@allow</code> covers commands and queries only (L1253), so any module may publish any contract&#39;s event, <code>approvals.answered</code> included, with any <code>by</code>.</li><li><strong>A module with two contracts:</strong> L1303 says how &quot;a module that declares one contract&quot; offers its operations. Two contracts would make <code>module.op</code> and <code>module.event</code> ambiguous.</li><li><strong><code>main</code>&#39;s arguments:</strong> L865 uses <code>args |&gt; drop(1)</code>, but what is <code>args[0]</code> under <code>eye run DIR -- …</code>?</li><li><strong>Arithmetic on built-in types:</strong> L741–743 lists combinations &quot;that make sense&quot; without saying the list is complete. <ul><li><code>Date - Duration</code> is used at L1053 but isn&#39;t listed; <code>Duration / Int</code>, <code>Int * Duration</code> and <code>Duration - Duration</code> work.</li><li><code>Duration * Float</code> fails (<code>expected Int, found Float</code>) even though <code>1.5 h</code> is a literal.</li><li><strong>Fix:</strong> give a full table.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Slices:</strong> what type is <code>xs[2..5]</code>, and what does sharing storage mean under value semantics (L364–366)?</li><li><strong>Strings:</strong> <code>s.charAt(i)</code> returns <code>Option(Char)</code> (<code>None</code> at <code>s.end()</code>); L371 says &quot;gives the character there&quot;.</li><li><strong><code>if c then x</code> with no <code>else</code></strong> has type <code>()</code>. That&#39;s reasonable, but unstated.</li><li><strong>Record literals:</strong> a record&#39;s type can come from later use (<code>var counter = { hits: 0, last: None }</code> at L473 checks). L390–393 grants this to string literals only, and L351 suggests it&#39;s an error.</li><li><strong>Keywords:</strong> <code>then</code> is missing from §4&#39;s lists (L275–287). <code>grammar.js</code> L511 makes it contextual after <code>if</code>.</li><li><strong>The grammar sketch (§23)</strong> lacks: <ul><li><code>extern type</code> (L1771, L1777);</li><li><code>where</code> on <code>extern fn</code>, which §18 L1445 allows;</li><li><code>uses</code> on function types (L1845);</li><li><code>_</code> and tuple patterns as lambda parameters (L338, L1809).</li></ul></li><li><strong><code>@destructive</code> model tools</strong> pause &quot;until someone approves&quot; (L1565), but whom? <code>approval!</code> needs <code>from:</code>.</li><li><strong>Tests and approvals:</strong> the test helper <code>approve(what, by: who = &quot;test&quot;)</code> (<code>ir/builtin.eye</code> L194) will be refused once &quot;only <code>from</code> may answer&quot; lands, unless the job asked &quot;test&quot;.</li><li><strong><code>eye test</code> and <code>eye.toml</code>&#39;s <code>modules</code>:</strong> L1638–1639 runs &quot;the modules it imports from its own directory included&quot;. Are modules found through <code>modules</code> directories included?</li><li><strong>Secrets:</strong> which comes first, the environment variable or the file under <code>$EYE_SECRETS</code> (L1626–1627)?</li><li><strong>Approval identity:</strong> comparing as text (L1132) leaves open how a <code>UserId(&quot;paul&quot;)</code> compares with a Telegram chat id.</li></ul><h3 id="_3-unclear-or-ambiguous-rules-two-readings-each" tabindex="-1">3. Unclear or ambiguous rules (two readings each) <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-unclear-or-ambiguous-rules-two-readings-each" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3. Unclear or ambiguous rules (two readings each)&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Ranges, L360–361:</strong> &quot;fields <code>start</code> and <code>end</code>&quot;. Either <code>end</code> is always exclusive, or it is whatever was written (the code: <code>(0..=5).end == 5</code>). Is <code>5..2</code> empty or wrapping? It is empty for <code>Int</code> and wraps for times.</li><li><strong>L536–537:</strong> &quot;A lower-case name followed by <code>.</code> is a module&quot;. Either everywhere (which would make <code>w.read!()</code> a module path) or only in types. It should say &quot;in a type&quot;.</li><li><strong>L1608–1609:</strong> &quot;<code>every(15 min)</code> fires at each multiple of its interval since midnight UTC&quot;. Either it resets each midnight (then <code>every(7 min)</code> has a 5-minute gap at 00:00) or it counts since the epoch.</li><li><strong>L1184:</strong> <code>@deadline(13:40, in: zone)</code>. For a run that starts at 14:00, either today&#39;s 13:40 (it fails at once) or tomorrow&#39;s.</li><li><strong>L1114 and L1124:</strong> <code>poll!(…, fn { miniflux.ready!() })</code> &quot;keeps only its outcome&quot;. Either the inner <code>!</code> calls are steps, contradicting &quot;keeps only its outcome&quot;, or they aren&#39;t steps, contradicting &quot;<code>!</code> marks every recorded step&quot;.</li><li><strong>L1132:</strong> &quot;<code>by</code> must be the request&#39;s <code>from</code>, both as text&quot;. Either the <code>Show</code> form or the JSON form, and either way two different identity types can collide or never match.</li><li><strong>L812–816:</strong> whether a function type nested inside a parameter type (<code>Option(fn() -&gt; a)</code>, <code>List(fn() -&gt; a)</code> as in <code>race</code>) counts as a callback. <code>race</code> says yes; the rule is phrased about &quot;a parameter&#39;s function type&quot;.</li><li><strong>L911:</strong> &quot;each must stay inside the repository&quot;. What if the program isn&#39;t in git?</li><li><strong>L1365–1367:</strong> &quot;Reading a field that no recording contains is a compile error&quot;. Which recordings? The <code>from:</code> path is a string the test passes at run time, so the compiler can&#39;t know it without new rules.</li><li><strong>L1170:</strong> a job can be started &quot;from entity methods, from <code>main</code> and tests, and from jobs&quot;. What about plain functions? (They can, which is how the key rule is bypassed; see top 4.)</li></ul><h3 id="_4-examples" tabindex="-1">4. Examples <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-examples" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;4. Examples&quot;">​</a></h3><p>All of these are listed under top 9. In addition:</p><ul><li><strong>§13 L929–930:</strong> <code>let (user, orders) = both(…)</code>. The extracted file is checked as module level (&quot;a module-level let binds one name&quot;), which conflicts with L1766–1767: &quot;Every <code>eye</code> example … is a whole file or the statements of a function body&quot;. The tool should wrap statement-only examples, or the example should be marked.</li><li><strong>§15 L1149:</strong> <code>choices: [yes, no]</code> with <code>press.choice == yes.id</code> compares strings, while row 133 and §11 say choices should be matched as typed variants (<code>Admission.parse</code>).</li><li><strong>The fragments are fine as they are.</strong> ROADMAP C.7 says 4 of 33 examples check; there are 38 example files now, with 6 clean in isolation. The real errors are the ones in top 9.</li></ul><h3 id="_5-design-risks-against-the-pillars-and-how-to-decide" tabindex="-1">5. Design risks against the pillars and &quot;How to decide&quot; <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-design-risks-against-the-pillars-and-how-to-decide" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5. Design risks against the pillars and &quot;How to decide&quot;&quot;">​</a></h3><ol><li><strong>Entity methods with side effects</strong> (top 1). Example: <code>pub fn join(p) { …; notify.send(owner, &quot;joined&quot;, key: …) }</code> compiles today, runs inside the transaction, and repeats on retry.</li><li><strong>Dropped outcomes</strong> (top 7). The tour&#39;s own <code>notify.send!(…)</code> drops <code>Refused</code> and <code>Uncertain</code>.</li><li><strong>Nominal record literals in lambdas and pipelines.</strong> The obvious data program fails:<div class="language-eye vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">eye</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">let</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> rows </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> users </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">|&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> map</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">fn</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> u: { name: u.name, age: u.age })   </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># error: which record type?</span></span></code></pre></div>§11&#39;s own <code>buttons</code> example fails the same way. Q25 is open; this is evidence that it hurts ordinary code. Options: let a lambda&#39;s body take the element type from where the result goes, or allow <code>User { … }</code> at the literal.</li><li><strong>Closures can&#39;t read a <code>var</code></strong> (L411, L491) [verified]. This is a very common loop shape:<div class="language-eye vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">eye</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">var</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> seen: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">Set</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">String</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">) </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> {}</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">for</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> item </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">in</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> feed {</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">  let</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> fresh </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> item.links </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">|&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> filter</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">fn</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> l: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">!</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(seen </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">|&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> contains</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(l)))   </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># error: copy into a let</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">  seen </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> seen </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">+</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> toSet</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(fresh)</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">}</span></span></code></pre></div>Under value semantics, capturing the current <em>value</em> is sound. Allow reading a <code>var</code> in a callback that doesn&#39;t escape, or capture by value. (Rule 6 in &quot;How to decide&quot;: strict, but clumsy here.)</li><li><strong>Division needs <code>NonZero</code>.</strong> <code>sum / (xs |&gt; size)</code> needs a <code>match nonzero(…)</code> every time an average is taken. Consider a <code>div</code> that returns an <code>Option</code>, or <code>/</code> on <code>NonZero</code> produced by <code>size</code> of a non-empty list.</li><li><strong>A rule shaped by the interpreter.</strong> L1067–1068 says &quot;a loop that never ends belongs in the job&#39;s body, not in a function it calls once&quot;. The natural refactor (extracting <code>watchLoop!()</code>) makes a run grow without bound, and compiled code won&#39;t have this limit. Mark it as a limitation of the interpreter, not a language rule, or save the frames of called functions too.</li><li><strong>Approvals&#39; safety rests on the routing job&#39;s honesty.</strong> The obvious shortcut <code>approvals.answered.publish!({ id: r.id, approved: true, by: r.from })</code> passes the <code>by == from</code> check. Combined with events having no <code>@allow</code>, any module can grant consent. Consider a typed identity supplied by the contract that delivers the press, rather than text the program writes.</li><li><strong>Concurrency in jobs is unvalidated.</strong> Tasks in jobs &quot;only compute&quot; (L976–979), so fetching 20 feeds in parallel in a job needs 20 child runs and <code>all!</code>, which isn&#39;t built. The lab uses no concurrency at all (<code>parallelMap</code>, <code>both</code>, <code>all!</code> and <code>first!</code> have no hits in <code>testdata/lab</code>). This needs a real workload before it is settled.</li><li><strong><code>main</code> runs again on every start</strong> (L883–890). Only direct, keyless job starts are guarded. Entity calls and publishes repeat, and so do starts through a helper. Example: <code>pub fn main() { Ledger(&quot;x&quot;).credit(10) }</code> credits on every restart.</li><li><strong>Hidden memory cost of slices.</strong> Slices &quot;share storage&quot; (L364), so a 10-byte slice keeps a 100 MB <code>Bytes</code> alive. Pillar: lean on memory, costs visible.</li><li><strong>Overloaded names</strong> cause real collisions: <ul><li><code>end</code>: a string offset, a range field, ending an entity;</li><li><code>start</code>: a job, a string, a range;</li><li><code>first</code> and <code>all</code>: collection functions, and the waits <code>first!</code>/<code>all!</code> (status: &quot;resolved as the collection functions&quot;);</li><li><code>join</code>: joining strings, and <code>run.join!()</code>;</li><li><code>version</code>: <code>@version</code> and the snapshot field (and a state field);</li><li><code>Handler</code>: three meanings. <code>first!</code> breaks the rule that <code>!</code> only marks the same call as a step. Add a glossary and rename the waits (e.g. <code>allDone!</code>, <code>firstDone!</code>).</li></ul></li><li><strong>Three timeout conventions:</strong> <code>poll!</code> gives <code>false</code>, <code>next!</code> gives <code>None</code>, <code>approval!</code> gives <code>Expired</code>. Pick one shape.</li><li><strong>Function types mean different things by position</strong> (contradiction above). This trips readers and agents.</li><li><strong>Strings count characters:</strong> <code>size</code>, <code>take</code> and <code>drop</code> on strings cost O(n) with no sign at the call. This is minor.</li></ol><h3 id="_6-open-questions-1–34" tabindex="-1">6. Open questions 1–34 <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-open-questions-1–34" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;6. Open questions 1–34&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>State</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Time conditions in views: <strong>open</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Coherence: <strong>decided</strong> (row 101). The text &quot;decided… no&quot; no longer states the question; rephrase or drop it.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Exposing entities and jobs as contracts: <strong>open</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Job code upgrades: <strong>decided and built</strong> (row 132, status ✓). Drop it, keeping the &quot;migrate for resume points&quot; note as a new question if wanted.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Packages: <strong>partly decided</strong> (<code>eye.toml</code> root and <code>modules</code>); lock and registry still <strong>open</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>REPL and jobs: <strong>decided and built</strong>. Drop it.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Macros after v1.0: <strong>parked</strong>, not a question for now. Move it to a &quot;later&quot; list.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Multi-line strings: <strong>decided and built</strong>. Drop it.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Journal: <strong>decided and built</strong>. The long-runs bullet says &quot;decided and done&quot;. Only the retention settings remain <strong>open</strong>; the multi-runner bullet duplicates Q28. Trim it.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Streaming model output: <strong>open</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Prompt template files: <strong>open</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Request-scoped values via <code>with</code>: <strong>open</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td><code>eye fmt</code> and the two <code>if</code> forms: <strong>open</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td><code>if let</code>: <strong>open</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Typed choices: <strong>decided and built</strong>. Labels (<code>##</code> or <code>Show</code>) still <strong>open</strong>; keep only that part.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>What a fake received: <strong>decided and built</strong>. &quot;Unused exchanges fail a test?&quot; still <strong>open</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Approvals: <strong>decided, not built</strong> (#71/C.8). Say so.</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Contracts shared between programs: <strong>open</strong>, folded into Q5.</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Which actions are effects: <strong>decided and built</strong> (row 129). Drop it, but see top 4: starting a job is still not an effect.</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>Collection function types: <strong>decided</strong>. The code additionally lets them be called as methods (top 10).</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td><code>extern type</code> replaces <code>Handle</code>: <strong>decided</strong> (the checker is built; nothing runs foreign code yet). Drop it.</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>Printing in jobs: <strong>decided and built</strong>. Drop it.</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>Where <code>!</code> goes on a helper: <strong>open</strong>, and it depends on defining waiting functions (top 5).</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td><code>Self</code> and <code>dyn Store</code>: <strong>open</strong>, but its premise is <strong>stale</strong> (row 110 has no store example).</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>Naming a record&#39;s type at its literal: <strong>open</strong>, and the spec&#39;s own §11 example now argues for it.</td></tr><tr><td>26</td><td>What <code>@memory</code> measures: <strong>open</strong>. The attribute table contradicts it.</td></tr><tr><td>27</td><td>One flag for the store: <strong>open</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>28</td><td>Several runners on one database: <strong>open</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>29</td><td>Shape of task groups: <strong>open</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>30</td><td>Model prices and routing: <strong>open</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>31</td><td><code>Option</code> parameters with defaults: <strong>open</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>32</td><td>Time-travel debugging: <strong>open</strong> (wanted, no task). It is also wrongly cited by row 117.</td></tr><tr><td>33</td><td>Effects of a function that is only stored: <strong>open</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>34</td><td>Online playground: <strong>open</strong> (wanted, no task).</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Missing questions to add:</strong> ending entities; re-entrant calls; must-use results; reading entities and views in jobs; the effect of starting a job; whether <code>uses</code> is required on entity methods and jobs.</p><h3 id="_7-structure-and-writing" tabindex="-1">7. Structure and writing <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-structure-and-writing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7. Structure and writing&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>The decision log has 60 rows of very mixed size.</strong> CLI flags, environment-variable names and lab history sit in the &quot;Why&quot; column, and the longest rows run about 600 characters. Split it into core language decisions and runtime/tooling decisions, and move the &quot;From the lab&quot; histories to the design pages.</li><li><strong>The open questions are mostly closed.</strong> About 14 of the 34 are decided. Move decided ones into the log, keep their residual parts as new numbered questions, and stop renumbering (row 117&#39;s stale reference shows the cost).</li><li><strong>Implementation detail sits in normative text:</strong> §15 &quot;Where runs live&quot; (buckets of 64, advisory locks, schemas), §19 provider environment variables, the dates in row 131 and §15. Move these to <code>docs/internals</code> and leave rules in SPECS.</li><li><strong>§21 is obsolete</strong> and <strong>§22 duplicates ROADMAP</strong> and is partly stale. Keep §22&#39;s parsing decision and point to ROADMAP and compilation.md for the rest.</li><li><strong>Version tags:</strong> &quot;From v0.2&quot; (rows 109–110, L546, L948, L1842) tags things already built before v0.2 ships, while row 96&#39;s v0.2 feature (SQL) isn&#39;t built. Use status.md for &quot;what&#39;s built&quot; and keep SPECS free of version tags, or keep them accurate.</li><li><strong>There is no glossary.</strong> &quot;Checkpoint&quot; means a job save point and also zeropod&#39;s operation. &quot;Run&quot;, &quot;step&quot;, &quot;key&quot;, &quot;version&quot; and &quot;Handler&quot; each need one definition.</li><li><strong>The tour</strong> should say what it takes from <code>worlds.eye</code>, and the examples should use one Notify contract consistently.</li><li><strong>Outside SPECS but found on the way:</strong><ul><li>status.md L225 has two table rows joined on one line (<code>…durable_test.go) || \\</code>sleep!`…<code>), so the </code>sleep!` row doesn&#39;t render.</li><li><code>check/prelude.eye</code> L98 mentions <code>Handle</code>.</li><li><code>grammar.js</code> L823 says &quot;a function type without <code>uses</code> is pure&quot;.</li></ul></li></ul><hr><p><strong>Files referred to:</strong> <code>SPECS.md</code>, <code>docs/reference/status.md</code>, <code>ROADMAP.md</code>, <code>ir/builtin.eye</code>, <code>check/prelude.eye</code>, <code>check/std/{fs,approvals}.eye</code>, <code>check/runtime/store.eye</code>, <code>interp/jobs.go</code>, <code>testdata/programs/tour/worlds.eye</code>, <code>tree-sitter-eyelang/grammar.js</code>.</p>',52)])])}const p=t(n,[["render",d]]);export{g as __pageData,p as default};
